# 0x2F hosted endpoints — one box, two origins.
#
# The client origin and the relay MUST stay on separate hostnames: the E2E
# guarantee ("a compromised relay can neither read nor forge phone <-> Mac
# traffic") holds only while the pairing page + web client are served from an
# origin the relay process does not control. If the relay served the client,
# a compromised relay could substitute a pairing page that captures the code
# the user types and derive the E2E key.
#
# Replace both hostnames with your own, then point the CLI at them:
#
#   export 0X2F_RELAY_URL=https://relay.your.domain
#   export 0X2F_CLIENT_ORIGIN=https://app.your.domain
#
# Caddy terminates TLS (Let's Encrypt) and serves the static client; the relay
# process only ever sees plain HTTP on 127.0.0.1. The Mac connects outbound,
# so no inbound ports, NAT rules, or static IPs are needed.

app.0x2f.dev {
	root * /srv/0x2f/client
	encode gzip
	file_server
}

relay.0x2f.dev {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
